\title{Dhuwal Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Dhuwal (ISO 639-3 dwu) is an Australian Aboriginal language of north-east Arnhem Land, part of the Yolngu Matha group. Searches found little material labelled specifically as Dhuwal; most resources cover Yolngu Matha broadly or other varieties (for example Djambarrpuyngu). Nothing found shows Dhuwal-specific school materials.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No Dhuwal-specific primers or textbooks were found. One related item is a self-published illustrated children's dictionary in Djambarrpuyngu, a different variety, reported as used at Milingimbi school: Grace Tongatua, \url{https://leanpub.com/u/balandamiyalk}.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Heath, \emph{Dhuwal (Arnhem Land) texts on kinship and other subjects, with grammatical sketch and dictionary} (scholarly): \url{https://deepblue.lib.umich.edu/items/a6721d13-a93d-471c-b05d-bf641a92f3bc}
\item Glottolog reference record for a Yolngu Matha dictionary that lists Dhuwal: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/5691}
\item Charles Darwin University online Yolngu dictionary (announcement): \url{https://www.cdu.edu.au/news/news-archive/cdu-launches-first-online-yolngu-dictionary}
\item CDU Yolngu resources page: \url{https://www.cdu.edu.au/centres/macp/resources_maratja.html}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item English--Yolngu Matha Legal Dictionary (ARDS; adult/legal domain, copyright restricted): \url{https://ards.com.au/s/English-Yolngu-Matha-Legal-Dictionary.pdf}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Yolnu Matha health resources, an app covering Dhuwal, Dhuwala and Dhangu with audio and pictures (NT Health library): \url{https://library.health.nt.gov.au/mobileresources/YolnuMatha}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The reference description says Dhuwal is not known to be taught in schools. Related bilingual programmes exist in Yolngu communities, but their relation to Dhuwal was not established here. A curriculum would need to build on the dictionary and text material above and on work with Dhuwal speakers.
\end{document}
