\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Dawawa (ISO 639-3 dww; Glottocode dawa1242) is an Austronesian language of Milne Bay Province, Papua New Guinea, spoken around Cape Vogel by roughly 2,000--2,500 people. Little beyond a Bible translation was found in search results.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia overview of the language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawawa_language}
\item Glottolog language record: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/dawa1242.bigmap.html}
\item Glosbe translation pages (user-generated, quality unverified): \url{https://glosbe.com/dww/dbl}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Vari Verenama}, Dawawa scripture text on eBible: \url{https://ebible.org/dww/FRT01.htm}
\item Joshua Project people-group profile: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11515/PP}
\item Joshua Project language page: \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/languages/dww}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found beyond the online scripture text.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Dawawa is reported as a first language of its community and not known to be taught in schools. The scripture text is the only substantial written material found, so a curriculum would need new materials developed with the community.
\end{document}
