\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Bhutanese Sign Language (BhSL, ISO 639-3 dyl) is the sign language of Bhutan's Deaf community. Its development began with the Deaf Education Unit, established in 2003, now the Wangsel Institute for the Deaf in Paro.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wangsel Institute for the Deaf, about page: \url{https://wangsel.education.gov.bt/about/}
\item Wangsel Institute history: \url{https://wangsel.education.gov.bt/history/}
\item Paro district page on the institute: \url{https://paro.gov.bt/about-us/institutions/wangsel-institute-for-the-deaf/}
\end{itemize}

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Bhutanese Sign Language (BhSL) page, Wangsel Institute: \url{https://wangsel.education.gov.bt/bhutan-sign-language/}
\item UNICEF: Deaf students construct first sign language for Bhutan: \url{https://www.unicef.org/bhutan/stories/deaf-students-construct-first-sign-language-bhutan}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Disabled People's Organization of Bhutan: \url{https://dpobhutan.org/?p=3620}
\item BhSL interpreting course launched by Wangsel and DPOB: \url{https://wangsel.education.gov.bt/bhutanese-sign-language-interpreting-course-launched-by-wangsel-institute-for-the-deaf-and-dpob/}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No sign-language video dictionary or corpus was found in search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Wangsel teaches using English alongside BhSL. In July 2025 its faculty were reported to have been trained at Mahidol University's Ratchasuda Institute in sign language curriculum design for kindergarten through secondary (\url{https://www.rama.mahidol.ac.th/international/news/event/18072025-1324}). Reports note a lack of interpreters and support after Class XII (\url{https://asianews.network/bhutanese-students-with-disabilities-struggle-for-access-to-higher-education/}).
\end{document}
